From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Philip Mueller <claude@philipmueller.ch>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 10:30:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(apertus2): answer a continuation after a truncated burst
 with a context-length error

Cause: with output_format apertus2 and Gym token capture in token_in mode,
each request extends the exact tokens of the previous call through
extend_chat_prompt, which asserted that the previous burst ended at
<|wait|>. A burst cut off at a token limit has no <|wait|>, so the next
request raised AssertionError; the HTTP handler's catch-all turned it into
HTTP 500, which Gym retries. Separately, an Apertus 2 prompt was checked
against max_model_len only when the request set max_tokens: vLLM's own
check sees only the placeholder render, so an over-long prompt without
max_tokens was never rejected by NeMo-RL.

Fix: extend_chat_prompt raises TruncatedBurstError (a ValueError) when the
last burst is non-empty and contains no <|wait|>, i.e. generation hit
max_model_len or max_new_tokens. preprocess_chat maps it to the same
VLLMValidationError as _clamp_max_tokens, with "maximum context length" in
the message, so the handler answers HTTP 400 invalid_request_error and
calls _abort_request_capture(reason="context_length") once. The call fails
before begin_call, so nothing is staged for it. Any other prefix that does
not end at <|wait|> still fails the assert. _clamp_max_tokens now always
checks that the prompt leaves room, also without max_tokens.

Gym's vllm_model turns a 400 with "context length" into an empty
completion with finish_reason "length" and does not retry it; Terminus-2
then raises ContextLengthExceededError and ends the trial, which is still
verified. The uncommitted call is recorded with
request_finished_without_staged_coordinates, which NeMo-RL does not count
as poisoning, so the rollout's committed calls stay usable.

Production evidence: job 5303503 logged 243 such AssertionErrors (81 Gym
requests x 3 retries) in 5 rollouts; 4 of the 5 scored 0, losing ~70-90 s
each.

Signed-off-by: Philip Mueller <claude@philipmueller.ch>
